  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft behind the trough digging into the\nGreat Lakes, and that pattern will continue into the upcoming\nwork week. Weak embedded shortwaves producing light rain showers\nwill stay to the northeast of our counties, with a dry period\nexpected. After tomorrow morning, temps will be on a general\nwarming trend as the upper trough pulls further east and heights\nrise. A bit of a weak cold front mid-week, but warming back up\nagain Friday and into the weekend as flow starts to become more\nsouthwesterly. Some of the ensemble members try to bring out a\nshortwave trough and some precip on Sunday, but uncertainty in\nthat pattern change is still high.\n\n...Frosty temperatures tonight...\n\nSurface high pressure extending from Manitoba into the Dakotas\nwill begin to shift eastward tonight. Cloud cover from the\nreinforcing shortwave currently digging into northeastern MN\nwill also shift eastward, although can't completely rule out\nsome more cirrus coming in from the west towards morning. Best\nchances of temps bottoming out below 36 degrees are in the Lake\nof the Wood region and in west central MN. NBM has probabilities\nof getting below 36 of 60 to 70 percent in some areas, but at\nthis point 32 and below seems unlikely. Most of that area is\nboggy and unlikely to have impacts, but there are some areas in\nthe western portions of the advisory area that have more\npopulation. Further west into the Red River Valley and eastern\nND, there will be more wind as winds start to pick up from the \nsoutheast as the high pulls away. Not completely ruling out\nfrost formation particularly near the Canadian border, but not\nconfident enough to include quite yet.",
